IMPORTANT — THE APACHE-2.0 GRANT COVERS ONLY WHAT THE GRANTOR OWNED.

An Apache-2.0 LICENSE file at the root of a repository is a grant by that
repository's copyright holder over that holder's own work. It does not, and
cannot, relicense third-party material the holder bundled in. Sections 2 to 5
below list material inside `vendor/upstream-exchange/` that the upstream author did
not write and had no evident right to sublicense to us. Each is assessed on its
own terms, not on the root LICENSE file.

Direct evidence that this tree has been re-badged at least once, which is why
the above matters in practice rather than in theory:

  vendor/upstream-exchange/01_wallet_rpc/act/bin/ contained 8 committed `.class`
  files under the package root `com/ztuo/bc/wallet/`, while the `.java` sources
  beside them are `com/bizzan/bc/wallet/`. The compiled output was from a build
  of the same code under a different vendor identity. Whatever chain of custody
  carried this module here, it passed through at least one party other than the
  named upstream, and no licence record accompanied that step.

  PAST TENSE, and deliberately so: that directory was removed on branch
  `feat/spine-wallet-perimeter` (board item A1.4). It was Eclipse build output —
  referenced by no POM, no module list and no build step — and it also carried a
  stale `application.properties` holding a MongoDB credential and a compiled copy
  of the wallet controller from before the RPC auth wall landed in #86.

  The finding above is not weakened by the removal. The evidence is preserved in
  git history, where the `.class` files and their `com/ztuo/` package root can
  still be read, and the observation is recorded here. Keeping stale bytecode in
  the working tree to serve as an exhibit is not how evidence is retained; it is
  just how a credential survives a cleanup.


